<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heidelberg University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1386">1386</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as University of Heidelberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> University was founded in 1386 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupert-i-elector-palatine">Rupert I, Elector Palatine</a>, and is the oldest university in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was a centre of humanism and, after the Electoral Palatinate became Calvinist, of Reformed theology, associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> of 1563. When Heidelberg passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, Elector Charles Frederick reorganised the university in 1803, and it is named after him and Rupert.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university.jpg" alt="Heidelberg University" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uni Heidelberg; derivative work by Tou-x · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_University_of_Heidelberg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
